{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"040b36b2-b2a1-428e-a4b7-5bc973864b76","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The issue causing `run-vibe.sh` to exit within a second has been diagnosed and fixed, and the full explanation is documented in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-o8YNfk/ANSWER.md).\n\n### Summary of Cause \u0026 Fix\n\n1. **Cause:**\n   Even though OpenRouter is configured as the active provider, Mistral Vibe CLI 3.1.0 performs an initial check for `MISTRAL_API_KEY` at startup. Because `run-vibe.sh` runs with a clean environment (`env -i`) without `MISTRAL_API_KEY`, Vibe attempted to look up credentials in the macOS Keychain by running `/usr/bin/security`. The macOS sandbox ([agent.sb](file:///tmp/h-o8YNfk/agent.sb)) forbids `/usr/bin/security` execution (`deny process-exec`), resulting in an immediate failure with exit code 2:\n   ```text\n   Reading API keys from the macOS Keychain (set them as environment variables to skip this)\n   Error: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted: '/usr/bin/security'\n   ```\n\n2. **Fix:**\n   Updated [run-vibe.sh](file:///tmp/h-o8YNfk/run-vibe.sh) to include `MISTRAL_API_KEY=\"unused\"` on the `env -i` execution line alongside `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`. This satisfies Vibe's initial credential check and prevents it from invoking `/usr/bin/security`, while keeping the sandbox profile strictly intact.\n\n3. **Colleague's Note Evaluation:**\n   Details on which parts of the colleague's note applied and which were ignored have been recorded in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-o8YNfk/ANSWER.md).\n","duration_seconds":76.38229,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":279403,"output_tokens":2461,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"total_tokens":281864}}}
